  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14093453/assets/23d106faa9e3a72b27acc170cfb8ef2545465be7a80e88f833e6d20c654d8f64.png)\n\nWhich of the following diagrams depicts the lines representing the principal axes of the quadratic curve shown above?",
  "worked_solution": "The principal axes of a curve define a coordinate system in which the curve can be represented by its (simplest) canonical equation. \n\nOur curve is an ellipse. This curve will have the canonical equation of the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1\n$$\n\nif the axes pass through the opposite vertices of the ellipse.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14093453/assets/686c221d813d00c6bda5d9f1762fd8b327f85c8bc86a6e05aed5239028a896ba.png)",
